  "image_name": "0b0514870f21d03603dc3405322cac8b.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two ways of visualizing specific genomic regions in cells and shows that both feed into the same kind of distance-based image analysis. On the left, DNA-FISH is presented as a fixed-cell method: cells are chemically fixed, permeabilized, and the DNA is denatured so a fluorescent probe can hybridize to a target sequence. Two imaging variants are illustrated, including 3D-FISH and cryo-FISH, with cryo-FISH appearing to use a very thin section of about 150 nm.\n\nOn the right, CRISPR-based live-cell imaging is shown as an alternative for intact, living cells. A fluorescently tagged dCas9 guided by an sgRNA binds the target DNA region, allowing imaging without fixation or denaturation. This emphasizes the contrast between static labeling in processed cells and dynamic labeling in living cells.\n\nIn the center, the outputs of these methods are summarized as fluorescent spots whose spatial separations can be measured. A simple plot indicates comparison of distances between locus pairs, with shorter distances associated with “interacting” regions and larger distances with “non-interacting” ones. Overall, the figure explains how different labeling strategies are used to detect chromosome organization by quantifying distances between genomic loci.",
